<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
    Strata's privacy manifest.

    Apple's definition of "collect", verbatim from the App Privacy Details
    page, is the test every entry below is held to:

        "Collect" refers to transmitting data off the device in a way that
        allows you and/or your third-party partners to access it for a period
        longer than what is necessary to service the transmitted request in
        real time. ... Data that is processed only on device is not "collected"
        and does not need to be disclosed.

    Two words in it decide everything: "you" is the developer, and "third-party
    partners" is, on the same page, the analytics tools, ad networks, SDKs and
    other external vendors whose code you added to your app. Apple's own
    iCloud, reached through the system's CloudKit framework, is neither.

    UNTIL CREWS (2026-10-02) this list was empty, and that was right: wins live
    in SwiftData on the device, photographs live in the app's own container,
    and there is no server, no account, no analytics and no third-party SDK.
    CLGeocoder asking Apple what a coordinate is called is a real-time request
    Apple services and we never retain.

    This file once declared HealthAndFitness and PhotosOrVideos. Both were
    wrong, and wrong in the direction that hurts: the product page's privacy
    label told people Strata collects their health data and their photographs
    when it collected neither. A label is a statement to users; an untrue one
    is worse than a missing one, in EITHER direction.

    CREWS (spec docs/superpowers/specs/2026-10-02-crews-design.md, section 9;
    research docs/research/concept-and-social.md 4.3 and 4.5). What leaves
    the phone, and whether it is "collected":

    1. A crew's records (Crew, Member, SharedWin in Strata/Social/
       CrewRecords.swift: the crew's name, picture and time zone; your
       Profile name and your head's small pictures; a win's title, colour,
       icon, size, re-encoded photo, framing and times) are written to one
       custom zone in the PRIVATE iCloud database of whoever started the
       crew, and shared with its members through a CKShare whose
       publicPermission is .none. The developer cannot read a user's private
       or shared database: the CloudKit console shows the developer only
       their own iCloud account's private data, and the app has no server
       to copy anything to. Apple is not a third-party partner in Apple's
       own sense of the phrase. So none of it is "collected", and Name,
       PhotosOrVideos, OtherUserContent and UserID are NOT declared for it.
       The research doc reached the same reading (4.5, "arguably stays not
       collected", marked as inference to confirm). The spec's section 9.3
       listed those four as linked; declaring them would put "Some Wins
       collects your photos and your name" on the product page, which is
       the same kind of false statement this file was cleaned of on
       2026-09-10. If Apple's guidance or review says otherwise, add them
       here AND in App Store Connect together
       (docs/crews-app-store-privacy.md has the exact answers either way).

    2. A REPORT IS collected, and is declared below. CrewSafety.report
       writes a `Report` record to the app's PUBLIC CloudKit database, and
       that record type is readable only by the developer (set in the
       CloudKit dashboard, docs/crews-app-store-privacy.md). The developer
       reads it, for as long as it takes to act on it, which is the
       definition. It carries:
           crew, winID          random ids of the crew and the win
           sender, reporter     the random profile ids (ProfileStore.profileID)
                                of the person reported and the person reporting
           reason, title        the reason picked from a fixed list, and the
                                reported win's title (words a person wrote)
       plus CloudKit's own creatorUserRecordID, the reporter's app-scoped
       iCloud user record id. AND, corrected 2026-10-06 against the code
       (CrewSafety.send): the reported photograph or doodle as a CKAsset
       ("photo"), and, for a reported person, their first name in "title".
       So Photos or Videos and Name are declared below. No head.

    3. Not collected: the notification. iCloud sends a silent push and the
       app writes the notification on the phone (CrewNotifications.swift);
       the push token goes to Apple, never to the developer. The age range
       from Declared Age Range and the Sensitive Content Analysis verdict on
       a photo stay on the phone (CrewAge, CrewSafety.photoIsFine).

    If a server, an account, a backup or an analytics SDK is ever added, this
    is the first file to change and the App Store Connect answers are the
    second: they have to agree.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSPrivacyTracking</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSPrivacyTrackingDomains</key>
	<array/>
	<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypes</key>
	<array>
		<!--
		    The reporter's and the reported person's profile ids, and the
		    reporter's CloudKit user record id, on a Report record.

		    LINKED: an id is by its nature linked to the person it names;
		    it is the whole point of the field, so the developer can see
		    that two reports concern the same sender.
		    NOT TRACKING: never combined with data from other companies'
		    apps or sites, never shared with a data broker. There is no
		    ad or analytics code in the app.
		    APP FUNCTIONALITY: Apple's purpose for "implement security
		    measures" and "customer support". A report exists so the
		    developer can act on abuse (guideline 1.2) and for nothing
		    else.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeUserID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		    The reported win's title and the reason chosen, on a Report
		    record. A title is words someone wrote; Apple's nearest type is
		    Other User Content. The photograph is declared on its own, below (missed until 2026-10-06).

		    LINKED: the title sits on the same record as the sender's
		    profile id. NOT TRACKING and APP FUNCTIONALITY: as above.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeOtherUserContent</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
			<!--
		    The reported photograph or doodle, attached to a Report as a
		    CKAsset when the thing reported has one (CrewSafety.send).
		    LINKED, NOT TRACKING, APP FUNCTIONALITY: as above.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePhotosorVideos</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		    A reported person's first name, in a person report's title
		    ("Person: Sam"). LINKED, NOT TRACKING, APP FUNCTIONALITY.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeName</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<true/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAppFunctionality</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		    ANONYMOUS USAGE (2026-10-08, Strata/Services/Analytics.swift): a random id made on
		    first launch, hashed, sent to TelemetryDeck. Never the device's own id.
		    NOT LINKED, NOT TRACKING, ANALYTICS (TelemetryDeck's own guidance).
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeDeviceID</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		    Which parts of the app are used (a win logged, a strip printed), never what
		    anyone wrote or photographed. NOT LINKED, NOT TRACKING, ANALYTICS.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeProductInteraction</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
		<!--
		    That a tip was bought, and which of the three. NOT LINKED, NOT TRACKING, ANALYTICS.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurchaseHistory</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeLinked</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypeTracking</key>
			<false/>
			<key>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposes</key>
			<array>
				<string>NSPrivacyCollectedDataTypePurposeAnalytics</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypes</key>
	<array>
		<!--
		    UserDefaults, for the app's own settings: the timer length, whether
		    photographs go to the camera roll, whether wins remember where they
		    were, and Crews' own (the last crews a win went to, people you
		    blocked, your age range). CA92.1 is "accessing information from
		    the app itself".

		    1C8F.1 is the App Group: SocialStore.groupDefaults keeps the crews
		    whose alerts you hid in UserDefaults(suiteName:
		    "group.JaydenBetts.Strata"), so a notification extension can read
		    it without the app running. CA92.1 does not cover a suite shared
		    with the group; 1C8F.1 is the reason that does. (This comment used
		    to say "there is no app group"; there has been one since the
		    widget.) No managed configuration.

		    Nothing else in the app touches a required-reason API. Audited:
		    no file-timestamp calls, no disk-space calls, no system-boot-time
		    calls, no active-keyboard calls.
		-->
		<dict>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPIType</key>
			<string>NSPrivacyAccessedAPICategoryUserDefaults</string>
			<key>NSPrivacyAccessedAPITypeReasons</key>
			<array>
				<string>CA92.1</string>
				<string>1C8F.1</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
</dict>
</plist>
